@status DRAFT 2026-11-05, not approved

! conclusion: The "militant" in militant pacifism means organisation, rigour and persistence on the side of peace; it never licenses force, and it must not rebuild a command apparatus to get there.

  + context: "Those of us who love peace must organize as effectively as the war hawks." (Martin Luther King Jr.). Deleuze asks for "a war-machine that would not recreate a State apparatus". The War Machine takes its name from that sentence.

  + IF:
    Peaceful work is left informal and under-resourced while coercive or adversarial work is well organised, peaceful work loses by default.

  + HOWEVER:
    Organising effectively often pulls toward central control, ranking and enforcement: the State apparatus Deleuze warns against. Martial vocabulary (tai chi's eight gates, war room) can also be misread as permission for force.

  + THEN:
    Bring the full discipline of the machine (typed evidence, review, persistence, coordination) to peaceful aims, while keeping authority distributed: agents propose and review, the operator assesses, refusals are respected. Read martial vocabulary as formation and energy, never as advocating violence.

  + BECAUSE:
    Effectiveness decides which aims are realised; distributed authority decides whether the means reproduce what the aims oppose.
